<DocsPage
  title="Writing workflows"
  description="A workflow is one Go function. Its sessions, loops, branches, and prompts are all visible where they happen."
  eyebrow="Feature"
  {headings}
>

<h2 id="whole">A whole workflow</h2>

This is the built-in `review` workflow. Nothing is left out.

<ReviewCode />

Record the goal in scope. Open a session for a role. Ask for a typed answer. Record the result. The prompt is a constant a few lines below; the goal reaches the agent because it was set in the scope the turn runs in.

<h2 id="bigger">A bigger one</h2>

The built-in `implement` workflow, condensed: a planner picks tasks, a coder does each under a coach, a command gathers evidence, and an independent validator decides when the promise is met.

<LoopCode />

<figure>
  <Shot src="shots/planner.png" alt="The console map of a planner loop with its planner selected." label="a recorded run of the same shape" />
  <figcaption>The same shape as a run. Planner, task scope, watched coding turn, commands, validator, and the arrow back.</figcaption>
</figure>

There is no workflow engine underneath. `range`, `break`, `if`, and `return` do what they do in any Go program. Retries, critique rounds, bake-offs, and delivery steps are code you write inline, because a tactic hidden in a helper is a tactic nobody can read.

<h2 id="rules">What keeps it readable</h2>

- **Prompts are compile-time constants** or embedded files. What an agent is sent is readable at the call site, never assembled somewhere else. Task data travels as scoped context.
- **Workflows name roles, never models.** `gimbal.RoleCodeReview` says what kind of thinking the step needs. A run binds it.
- **Answers are typed.** `Generate[Result]` sends the struct's JSON Schema to the harness and validates what comes back. Field comments are instructions to the agent.
- **Lifetimes follow `context.Context`.** Returning from a scope closes its sessions and stops its services. Cancelling the run interrupts the agents.
- **`gimbal lint`** catches authoring mistakes statically, standalone or as a `go vet` tool.

<h2 id="command">From function to command</h2>

`go generate` turns the entry function into a CLI command and a graph:

- one flag per field of the parameter struct, with the field's comment as help
- one model flag per role the workflow names, defaulting from one shared file
- the function's doc synopsis as short help, the package doc as long help
- a typed `workflow.Graph` literal, read from the function's source, that the console draws

<h2 id="shapes">Choosing a shape</h2>

| Need | Shape |
| --- | --- |
| One well-scoped task | One agent turn. |
| Dependent stages | A sequence, with explicit results passed onward. |
| Independent investigations | Concurrent branches in a `Group`, then a join. |
| Several agents need the same research | Research once, then `Fork` the conversation. |
| A known finite collection | `Iterate` over the items. |
| The next task depends on new evidence | A `PromiseLoop` with worker and validator feedback. |

Use the smallest shape that does the job.

</DocsPage>
